.Dd November 11, 2026
.Dt PKEYS 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pkeys
.Nd memory protection keys
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A protection key is a number from 0 to 15 that each page of user memory
carries, in bits 59 to 62 of its page table entry.
Each thread has a register, PKRU, with two bits for each key: bit 2k, the
access-disable bit, denies every data access to the pages of key k, and
bit 2k+1, the write-disable bit, denies writes to them.
A thread changes its own PKRU with the unprivileged
.Li wrpkru
instruction and reads it with
.Li rdpkru ,
so a program can open and close a region far faster than
.Xr mprotect 2
can, and for one thread alone.
The keys never refuse an instruction fetch.
.Pp
The x86-64 kernel turns the keys on at each processor's start where
the CPUID instruction
reports PKU, as Linux does: it sets CR4.PKE, so that the OSPKE bit of
leaf 7 reads 1 to a program, adds PKRU to XCR0, and
.Pa /proc/cpuinfo
lists
.Li pku
and
.Li ospke .
QEMU's
.Li max
processor under its emulator has them.
The i686 kernel never turns them on, as Linux's 32-bit kernel does not, and
answers the three calls with
.Er ENOSYS .
Without them the x86-64 kernel gives no key and answers the calls as Linux
does on such a processor; PKRU reads as 0.
.Ss Allocation
.Xr pkey_alloc 2
gives the lowest free key and sets its rights in the caller's PKRU,
.Xr pkey_free 2
frees one, and
.Xr pkey_mprotect 2
gives a range of pages a key.
Key 0 is the default of every page and starts allocated.
.Xr mprotect 2
and
.Xr mmap 2
with
.Dv PROT_EXEC
alone give the pages the execute-only key, which the kernel allocates the
first time and denies all data access in the PKRU of the thread that asked,
so the pages run but do not read.
The keys belong to the address space: threads share them, the child of
.Xr fork 2
copies them, and
.Xr execve 2
frees all but key 0.
.Ss PKRU
PKRU is part of the thread's extended state, saved and loaded at every
switch with the floating-point registers.
A new thread and the child of
.Xr fork 2
start with their creator's PKRU, and a program that
.Xr execve 2
starts begins with 0x55555554: key 0 open and every other key denied all
access.
.Pp
A signal handler runs with PKRU 0x55555554 as well.
The signal frame's XSAVE image holds the PKRU of the interrupted code,
with the PKRU bit of its XSTATE_BV set, and
.Xr sigreturn 2
loads it from there, so a handler may change it.
The kernel writes the frame with every key open, so a signal stack under a
denied key still takes the frame.
.Xr ptrace 2
reads and writes a tracee's PKRU in the
.Dv NT_X86_XSTATE
register set, and a core file's note of the same type holds it.
.Ss Faults
A data access that PKRU denies raises
.Dv SIGSEGV
with
.Va si_code
.Dv SEGV_PKUERR
(4),
.Va si_addr
the address and
.Va si_pkey
the page's key, also for a page not yet backed.
The key is checked before the page's protection, so a write to a
read-only page under a denied key is
.Dv SEGV_PKUERR
and under an open key
.Dv SEGV_ACCERR .
.Pp
The kernel honours the calling thread's PKRU when a system call reads or
writes its memory: a
.Xr read 2
into, or a
.Xr write 2
from, a page whose key the caller denies fails with
.Er EFAULT .
An access on another address space's behalf ignores the keys, as Linux's
does:
.Xr ptrace 2 ,
.Pa /proc/PID/mem
and
.Xr process_vm_readv 2
reach a denied page, even of the caller's own address space.
.Ss /proc
Where the keys are on, each region of
.Pa /proc/PID/smaps
has a
.Li ProtectionKey:
line with its key, before its
.Li VmFlags ;
where they are off there is none.
.Sh COMPATIBILITY
An i386 program on the x86-64 kernel gets the keys as a 64-bit one does,
as on Linux: the same calls, numbered 380 to 382, the same PKRU, and
.Va si_pkey
at its i386 offset.
.Pp
Guests of
.Pa /dev/kvm
are offered no keys: their CPUID lacks PKU, so CR4.PKE is reserved to
them, and the host's PKRU is saved before a guest runs and loaded again
after.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr mmap 2 ,
.Xr mprotect 2 ,
.Xr pkey_alloc 2 ,
.Xr ptrace 2 ,
.Xr sigaction 2 ,
.Xr proc 5
